<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>7: Finding potentials</title>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Let's consider the field <m>F(x,y) = (2xy,x^2)</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Verify that
  <m>\nabla\times F = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are two paths with the same initial and
  final points, explain why <m>\work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's find a potential function for <m>F</m> by choosing the basepoint <m>*
  = (0,0)</m>.  For a point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>, integrate over this curve <m>C</m>
  to define
  <md>
  f(x_0,y_0) = \work{C}{F}.
  </md></p>
        <p>\includegraphics{figures/07-curve.eps}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Verify that the function <m>f(x,y)</m> that you have found is a potential
  for <m>F</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the field
  <md>
  F(x,y) = \left(-\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}, \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\right).
  </md>
  We have seen that <m>\nabla\times F = 0</m> but that the field is not
  defined at the origin <m>(0,0)</m> so that the domain of the field is not
  simply connected.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's try to find a potential function by integrating.  Choose the
  basepoint to be <m>(1,0)</m>.  To define <m>f(x_0, y_0)</m>, integrate along
  the positive <m>x</m>-axis and then along a circular arc traversed in the
  counterclockwise direction.  What do you
  find for <m>f(2,0)</m>?  <m>f(0,2)</m>?  <m>f(-2,0)</m>?  <m>f(0,-2)</m>?</p>
        <p>\includegraphics{figures/07-radial.eps}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is a general expression for <m>f(x_0, y_0)</m>?  It may be
  helpful to think in terms of polar coordinates.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Why doesn't <m>f</m> define a potential function for <m>F</m> in the region
  where <m>F</m> is defined?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>C</m> is the curve shown below, what is <m>\work{C}{F}</m>?</p>
        <p>\includegraphics{figures/07-homotopy.eps}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now imagine the two radial pieces of <m>C</m> coming together and explain
  why <m>\work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m> where <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are the
  two circles below.</p>
        <p>\includegraphics{figures/07-homotopy-2.eps}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are the two curves shown below, explain why
  <m>\work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m>.</p>
        <p>\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{figures/07-homotopy-3.png}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>\work{C}{F}</m> if <m>C</m> is the curve below.</p>
        <p>\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{figures/07-homotopy-4.png}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
